Given an integer array nums, move all 0's to the end of it while maintaining the relative order of the non-zero elements.
Note that you must do this in-place without making a copy of the array.

Example 1:
Input: nums = [0,1,0,3,12]
Output: [1,3,12,0,0]

Example 2:
Input: nums = [0]
Output: [0]


Approach 1 (Brute Force)
CODE:
class Solution {
    public void moveZeroes(int[] nums) {

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {

            if (nums[i] == 0) {

                for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {

                    if (nums[j] != 0) {
                        int temp = nums[i];
                        nums[i] = nums[j];
                        nums[j] = temp;
                        break;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

Complexity:
Time - O(n)
Space - O(n)

Approach 2(Two pointers Swapping) (Optimal)
CODE:
class Solution {
    public void moveZeroes(int[] nums) {

        int j = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {

            if (nums[i] != 0) {

                int temp = nums[i];
                nums[i] = nums[j];
                nums[j] = temp;

                j++;
            }
        }
    }
}

Complexity:
TIme = O(n)
Space = O(1)


Approach 3 (Two pointers overwrite)(Cleaner and more efficient)

CODE:
class Solution {
    public void moveZeroes(int[] nums) {

        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != 0) {
                nums[count] = nums[i];
                count++;
            }
        }

        for (int i = count; i < nums.length; i++) {
            nums[i] = 0;
        }
    }
}

Complexity:
Time = O(n)
Space = O(1)
